1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,656
In 2018, Boeing delivered eight hundred and six airplanes,

2
00:00:04,656 --> 00:00:07,434
more than any other company on earth.

3
00:00:07,434 --> 00:00:11,940
That year, it kept ten and a half billion dollars as profit.

4
00:00:12,390 --> 00:00:14,267
Then the numbers turned.

5
00:00:14,267 --> 00:00:19,352
From 2019 to 2024, Boeing lost almost thirty-six billion dollars.

6
00:00:19,352 --> 00:00:25,063
In the same years, two crashes killed three hundred and forty-six people,

7
00:00:25,063 --> 00:00:28,740
a panel blew out of a brand-new jet in mid-air,

8
00:00:28,740 --> 00:00:33,590
and a strike shut its biggest factories for almost two months.

9
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:35,769
Here's a strange detail.

10
00:00:35,769 --> 00:00:38,435
When Boeing posted its loss for 2019,

11
00:00:38,435 --> 00:00:42,037
it was the company's first losing year since 1997.

12
00:00:42,037 --> 00:00:46,360
That is the year Boeing bought its rival, McDonnell Douglas.

13
00:00:46,810 --> 00:00:50,821
For most of a century, Boeing was the engineers' company.

14
00:00:50,821 --> 00:00:52,650
So how did it end up here?

15
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:54,572
Not with one bad airplane.

16
00:00:54,572 --> 00:00:58,140
With a slow change in what the company was trying to get right.

17
00:00:58,590 --> 00:01:02,590
Let's go back to when Boeing was run by people who built airplanes.

18
00:01:06,190 --> 00:01:13,127
In 1954, a prototype called the Dash 80 took off from Boeing Field in Seattle.

19
00:01:13,127 --> 00:01:15,350
It became the Boeing 707.

20
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:17,352
Nobody had ordered it.

21
00:01:17,352 --> 00:01:22,221
Boeing paid for it with its own money, about sixteen million dollars.

22
00:01:22,221 --> 00:01:25,396
One account says the boss persuaded the board

23
00:01:25,396 --> 00:01:28,288
to "bet every penny in Boeing's coffers".

24
00:01:28,288 --> 00:01:28,994
It worked.

25
00:01:28,994 --> 00:01:31,040
The 707 launched the jet age.

26
00:01:31,490 --> 00:01:34,733
Fifteen years later, Boeing did it again.

27
00:01:34,733 --> 00:01:40,428
In February 1969, the first 747 took off from Everett, north of Seattle.

28
00:01:40,428 --> 00:01:42,010
The first jumbo jet.

29
00:01:42,460 --> 00:01:47,368
And in the early nineties came the 777, the first jetliner designed

30
00:01:47,368 --> 00:01:52,350
entirely on computers, with eight airlines sitting in on the design.

31
00:01:52,350 --> 00:01:55,500
Boeing called the group "Working Together".

32
00:01:55,950 --> 00:01:58,076
The pattern was always the same.

33
00:01:58,076 --> 00:02:01,531
Spend a fortune up front, get the engineering right,

34
00:02:01,531 --> 00:02:03,790
and the money follows for decades.

35
00:02:04,240 --> 00:02:07,440
By the mid-nineties, a third planemaker was running out of road.

36
00:02:08,540 --> 00:02:10,885
McDonnell Douglas, based in St.

37
00:02:10,885 --> 00:02:15,045
Louis, built fighter jets and airliners like the MD-11.

38
00:02:15,045 --> 00:02:20,113
But by 1996, it won only about five percent of new airliner orders.

39
00:02:20,113 --> 00:02:24,500
Boeing had around sixty percent, Airbus about thirty-five.

40
00:02:24,950 --> 00:02:29,140
In December 1996, Boeing agreed to buy it in a stock

41
00:02:29,140 --> 00:02:33,007
deal worth thirteen point three billion dollars.

42
00:02:33,007 --> 00:02:36,230
The merger closed on August first, 1997.

43
00:02:36,680 --> 00:02:37,923
Boeing was the buyer.

44
00:02:37,923 --> 00:02:42,362
But many of the people who ended up running it came from McDonnell Douglas,

45
00:02:42,362 --> 00:02:46,920
above all Harry Stonecipher, who became president, and later chief executive.

46
00:02:47,370 --> 00:02:50,670
In 1998, Stonecipher told employees to "quit

47
00:02:50,670 --> 00:02:54,570
behaving like a family and become more like a team".

48
00:02:55,020 --> 00:02:59,140
Years later, in 2004, he said it even more clearly: "When people

49
00:02:59,140 --> 00:03:02,809
say I changed the culture of Boeing, that was the intent,

50
00:03:02,809 --> 00:03:07,380
so that it's run like a business rather than a great engineering firm."

51
00:03:07,830 --> 00:03:12,188
In 2001, Boeing moved its headquarters from Seattle to Chicago,

52
00:03:12,188 --> 00:03:15,853
helped by about sixty million dollars of tax credits.

53
00:03:15,853 --> 00:03:21,110
Its top executives now sat about seventeen hundred miles from the factories.

54
00:03:21,560 --> 00:03:23,008
None of this was unusual.

55
00:03:23,008 --> 00:03:25,962
Plenty of companies want to be run like a business.

56
00:03:25,962 --> 00:03:28,800
But in aviation, the engineering is the business.

57
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:35,783
The test was the 787 Dreamliner, a long-range jet made largely of carbon fiber.

58
00:03:35,783 --> 00:03:39,060
Boeing also wanted to build it in a new way.

59
00:03:39,510 --> 00:03:41,926
Instead of building most of the plane itself,

60
00:03:41,926 --> 00:03:45,201
it spread the work across more than fifty partners worldwide.

61
00:03:45,201 --> 00:03:49,550
By Boeing's own figure, partners would supply sixty-five percent of the airframe.

62
00:03:49,550 --> 00:03:53,630
Whole sections would arrive ready-made, and Boeing would snap them together.

63
00:03:54,080 --> 00:03:58,047
The idea was to put in less cash up front and share the risk.

64
00:03:58,047 --> 00:04:02,600
Instead, parts arrived late or unfinished, and Boeing had to fix them.

65
00:04:03,050 --> 00:04:05,503
The first 787 reached a customer,

66
00:04:05,503 --> 00:04:09,368
All Nippon Airways, in 2011, about three years late.

67
00:04:09,368 --> 00:04:14,498
One estimate put the program twenty-five billion dollars over budget.

68
00:04:14,498 --> 00:04:20,370
In January 2013, battery fires grounded the whole fleet for about three months.

69
00:04:20,820 --> 00:04:22,871
And it didn't end there.

70
00:04:22,871 --> 00:04:27,657
From 2021 to August 2022, Boeing stopped delivering 787s

71
00:04:27,657 --> 00:04:32,699
over manufacturing defects, like tiny gaps in the fuselage.

72
00:04:32,699 --> 00:04:38,340
About a hundred and twenty finished Dreamliners piled up, waiting.

73
00:04:38,790 --> 00:04:42,031
Meanwhile, money was going somewhere else.

74
00:04:42,031 --> 00:04:46,584
From 2013 to 2019, Boeing spent forty-three billion dollars

75
00:04:46,584 --> 00:04:51,214
buying back its own shares, according to a Fortune analysis.

76
00:04:51,214 --> 00:04:55,767
Over the same six years, it spent less than sixteen billion

77
00:04:55,767 --> 00:05:00,165
on research and development for its commercial airplanes.

78
00:05:00,165 --> 00:05:06,030
In 2016, it promised to hand back all of its free cash flow to shareholders.

79
00:05:06,480 --> 00:05:08,352
Buybacks are legal and common.

80
00:05:08,352 --> 00:05:11,347
But that money isn't spent on the next airplane.

81
00:05:11,347 --> 00:05:12,720
And Boeing needed one.

82
00:05:13,820 --> 00:05:19,762
Its best-selling plane, the 737, dated back to the 1960s.

83
00:05:19,762 --> 00:05:25,809
In 2010, Airbus put new, fuel-saving engines on its rival,

84
00:05:25,809 --> 00:05:30,500
the A320neo, and airlines rushed to order it.

85
00:05:30,950 --> 00:05:38,249
Boeing could design a brand-new airplane, which takes many years and many billions.

86
00:05:38,249 --> 00:05:43,437
Or put new engines on the old 737, faster and much cheaper.

87
00:05:43,437 --> 00:05:45,987
In 2011, it chose the second.

88
00:05:45,987 --> 00:05:52,670
One analyst later said, "Their hand was forced." That plane was the 737 MAX.

89
00:05:53,120 --> 00:05:57,130
The new engines were bigger, so they sat further forward,

90
00:05:57,130 --> 00:06:00,859
and changed how the plane handled in some situations.

91
00:06:00,859 --> 00:06:05,714
Boeing added software called MCAS to make it feel like the older 737.

92
00:06:05,714 --> 00:06:08,880
And it could be triggered by a single sensor.

93
00:06:09,330 --> 00:06:13,992
A congressional report later accused Boeing of "concealing

94
00:06:13,992 --> 00:06:17,850
the very existence of MCAS from 737 MAX pilots".

95
00:06:18,300 --> 00:06:22,987
In October 2018, a Lion Air 737 MAX crashed in Indonesia.

96
00:06:22,987 --> 00:06:27,344
All one hundred and eighty-nine people on board died.

97
00:06:27,344 --> 00:06:30,058
Five months later, in March 2019,

98
00:06:30,058 --> 00:06:34,251
an Ethiopian Airlines MAX crashed near Addis Ababa.

99
00:06:34,251 --> 00:06:37,540
One hundred and fifty-seven people died.

100
00:06:37,990 --> 00:06:39,830
Three hundred and forty-six people in total.

101
00:06:40,280 --> 00:06:42,865
Regulators grounded the MAX in March 2019.

102
00:06:42,865 --> 00:06:46,434
It stayed grounded for twenty months, until November 2020,

103
00:06:46,434 --> 00:06:50,680
after Boeing changed the software to read two sensors instead of one.

104
00:06:51,130 --> 00:06:56,593
In 2020, a US House committee called the crashes the "horrific culmination"

105
00:06:56,593 --> 00:07:01,838
of "faulty design and performance assumptions", "a lack of transparency"

106
00:07:01,838 --> 00:07:07,010
by Boeing's management, and failed oversight by the regulator, the FAA.

107
00:07:07,460 --> 00:07:10,657
By one count, the grounding cost Boeing about

108
00:07:10,657 --> 00:07:13,428
twenty billion dollars in direct costs.

109
00:07:13,428 --> 00:07:16,697
In 2021, the Justice Department charged Boeing

110
00:07:16,697 --> 00:07:19,681
with fraud over the plane's certification.

111
00:07:19,681 --> 00:07:24,726
In November 2025, a judge dropped the case at the department's request,

112
00:07:24,726 --> 00:07:28,633
after Boeing agreed to pay over a billion dollars more,

113
00:07:28,633 --> 00:07:31,191
including for the victims' families.

114
00:07:31,191 --> 00:07:35,454
The judge wrote that the deal "fails to secure the necessary

115
00:07:35,454 --> 00:07:39,220
accountability", but that he had no power to stop it.

116
00:07:39,670 --> 00:07:42,191
The MAX was flying again.

117
00:07:42,191 --> 00:07:44,510
Then came January 2024.

118
00:07:45,610 --> 00:07:49,667
On January fifth, 2024, Alaska Airlines Flight twelve eighty-two

119
00:07:49,667 --> 00:07:53,723
was climbing out of Portland, Oregon, at about fourteen thousand

120
00:07:53,723 --> 00:07:57,970
eight hundred feet, when a panel blew out of the side of the cabin.

121
00:07:58,420 --> 00:08:04,293
The panel was a door plug, covering an extra emergency exit this airline didn't use.

122
00:08:04,293 --> 00:08:06,740
The cabin lost pressure in seconds.

123
00:08:06,740 --> 00:08:09,817
The crew turned back and landed in Portland.

124
00:08:09,817 --> 00:08:11,075
No one was killed.

125
00:08:11,075 --> 00:08:15,340
Seven passengers and one flight attendant had minor injuries.

126
00:08:15,790 --> 00:08:17,505
The plane was almost new.

127
00:08:17,505 --> 00:08:20,592
Investigators found that the four bolts meant

128
00:08:20,592 --> 00:08:23,268
to hold the plug in place were missing.

129
00:08:23,268 --> 00:08:26,355
The plug was installed by Spirit AeroSystems,

130
00:08:26,355 --> 00:08:29,511
Boeing's fuselage supplier in Wichita, Kansas.

131
00:08:29,511 --> 00:08:34,725
At Boeing's factory in Renton, near Seattle, it was opened to repair rivets,

132
00:08:34,725 --> 00:08:38,430
and photos show it was closed again without the bolts.

133
00:08:38,880 --> 00:08:41,308
Boeing said there was no record of the work.

134
00:08:41,308 --> 00:08:44,398
Its chief executive, Dave Calhoun, said: "Whatever final

135
00:08:44,398 --> 00:08:48,040
conclusions are reached, Boeing is accountable for what happened."

136
00:08:48,490 --> 00:08:54,124
The next day, the FAA grounded a hundred and seventy-one 737 MAX 9s.

137
00:08:54,124 --> 00:09:01,001
It capped MAX production at thirty-eight a month, and its audit found Boeing hadn't

138
00:09:01,001 --> 00:09:07,713
complied with quality rules on how it controlled its processes and handled parts.

139
00:09:07,713 --> 00:09:14,010
The FAA's chief said Boeing "must commit to real and profound improvements".

140
00:09:14,460 --> 00:09:19,481
In June 2025, the safety board, the NTSB, blamed Boeing's failure to

141
00:09:19,481 --> 00:09:24,723
follow its own manufacturing procedures, and weak oversight by the FAA.

142
00:09:24,723 --> 00:09:29,375
Its chair, Jennifer Homendy, said: "the crew shouldn't have had

143
00:09:29,375 --> 00:09:34,100
to be heroes, because this accident never should have happened."

144
00:09:34,550 --> 00:09:35,823
One more twist.

145
00:09:35,823 --> 00:09:39,980
Spirit's Wichita plant used to be part of Boeing.

146
00:09:39,980 --> 00:09:45,070
Boeing sold it in 2005, and bought it back in December 2025.

147
00:09:46,170 --> 00:09:50,216
By March 2024, Calhoun had announced he would step down.

148
00:09:50,216 --> 00:09:56,069
In August, Boeing brought in an outsider, Kelly Ortberg, an engineer by training.

149
00:09:56,069 --> 00:09:59,610
He had barely arrived when the factories stopped.

150
00:10:00,060 --> 00:10:03,411
On September thirteenth, 2024, thirty-three thousand

151
00:10:03,411 --> 00:10:06,440
machinists walked out, in Seattle and Portland.

152
00:10:06,440 --> 00:10:11,724
They wanted big raises, and their old pension back, frozen about a decade earlier.

153
00:10:11,724 --> 00:10:13,980
The strike lasted fifty-three days.

154
00:10:14,430 --> 00:10:17,200
They went back in November with a thirty-eight

155
00:10:17,200 --> 00:10:20,272
percent raise over four years, but not the pension.

156
00:10:20,272 --> 00:10:25,270
Bank of America estimated the strike cost Boeing about fifty million dollars a day.

157
00:10:25,720 --> 00:10:26,999
Ortberg moved fast.

158
00:10:26,999 --> 00:10:31,979
He announced about seventeen thousand job cuts, and sold up to twenty-four

159
00:10:31,979 --> 00:10:36,353
billion dollars of new shares, to protect Boeing's credit rating.

160
00:10:36,353 --> 00:10:39,919
Five years earlier, Boeing was buying back its stock.

161
00:10:39,919 --> 00:10:41,400
Now it was selling it.

162
00:10:41,850 --> 00:10:45,819
For 2024, Boeing lost almost twelve billion dollars,

163
00:10:45,819 --> 00:10:49,330
and burned more than fourteen billion in cash.

164
00:10:50,430 --> 00:10:52,167
So where is Boeing now?

165
00:10:52,167 --> 00:10:57,075
In 2025, it delivered six hundred airplanes, its most since 2018.

166
00:10:57,075 --> 00:11:00,700
Airbus delivered seven hundred and ninety-three.

167
00:11:00,700 --> 00:11:06,590
But for the first time in seven years, Boeing won more new orders than Airbus.

168
00:11:07,040 --> 00:11:12,645
The FAA let it raise 737 production to forty-two a month in October 2025,

169
00:11:12,645 --> 00:11:16,560
and Boeing says it's now moving toward forty-seven.

170
00:11:17,010 --> 00:11:21,728
It even reported a profit for 2025, about two point two billion dollars,

171
00:11:21,728 --> 00:11:26,970
but only thanks to a nine point six billion dollar gain from selling a business.

172
00:11:27,420 --> 00:11:30,903
The latest numbers are from July to September 2026.

173
00:11:30,903 --> 00:11:34,113
Revenue: twenty-four point two billion dollars.

174
00:11:34,113 --> 00:11:36,844
Net loss: a hundred and sixteen million,

175
00:11:36,844 --> 00:11:40,122
down from more than five billion a year earlier.

176
00:11:40,122 --> 00:11:44,220
And it generated almost half a billion dollars of free cash.

177
00:11:44,670 --> 00:11:45,684
But look inside.

178
00:11:45,684 --> 00:11:50,692
Boeing's services business made about eighteen cents of profit on every dollar.

179
00:11:50,692 --> 00:11:53,544
The division that builds airliners still lost

180
00:11:53,544 --> 00:11:56,270
about six and a half cents on every dollar.

181
00:11:56,720 --> 00:11:59,878
Boeing owes about forty-six billion dollars,

182
00:11:59,878 --> 00:12:02,821
against about twenty-one billion in cash.

183
00:12:02,821 --> 00:12:08,348
And it has orders worth seven hundred and forty-five billion dollars waiting,

184
00:12:08,348 --> 00:12:12,440
including more than six thousand three hundred airplanes.

185
00:12:12,890 --> 00:12:17,584
The smallest and largest versions of the MAX are now certified.

186
00:12:17,584 --> 00:12:22,055
The new 777X, after a four point nine billion dollar charge,

187
00:12:22,055 --> 00:12:25,930
should reach its first customer in 2027, years late.

188
00:12:26,380 --> 00:12:30,460
As Ortberg put it in October: "But we are not done."

189
00:12:31,560 --> 00:12:33,854
So what really went wrong at Boeing?

190
00:12:33,854 --> 00:12:38,953
Not one decision, but a long series of them, each saving money or time at first:

191
00:12:38,953 --> 00:12:44,179
a merger that brought in a finance-first culture, a Dreamliner built by outsiders,

192
00:12:44,179 --> 00:12:48,640
an old jet with new engines, and billions handed back to shareholders.

193
00:12:49,090 --> 00:12:52,342
And each time, the bill came later, and bigger.

194
00:12:52,342 --> 00:12:54,626
Twenty billion for the grounding.

195
00:12:54,626 --> 00:12:57,394
Three years of delays on the Dreamliner.

196
00:12:57,394 --> 00:13:00,785
Almost thirty-six billion in losses in six years.

197
00:13:00,785 --> 00:13:05,490
And a cost no number can measure: three hundred and forty-six lives.

198
00:13:05,940 --> 00:13:09,399
In most industries, cutting costs makes a product cheaper.

199
00:13:09,399 --> 00:13:12,559
In aviation, cutting corners doesn't remove the cost.

200
00:13:12,559 --> 00:13:15,660
It moves it into the future, where it's much bigger.

201
00:13:16,110 --> 00:13:19,814
Boeing is now trying to pay that bill down, one airplane at a time.

202
00:13:19,814 --> 00:13:24,070
Whether it can, we'll know from the factory floor, not from the next quarter.
